WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:13.218
Hi! Angles are everywhere: the corner of a book, the hands of a clock, an open door. Today we'll learn how to name angles and how to measure and draw them with a protractor.

2
00:00:13.368 --> 00:00:21.858
An angle is made of two arms that meet at a point called the vertex. Here the vertex is B, and the arms pass through A and C.

3
00:00:22.008 --> 00:00:35.370
We write it as angle ABC, or angle CBA. The vertex, B, must always be in the middle. If a small letter is written inside the angle, we can also call it angle a.

4
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.970
Angles are measured in degrees, shown with a small circle. A protractor has two scales.

5
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:50.994
Step one: put the centre of the protractor on the vertex, B. Step two: line up the zero line with one arm, BC.

6
00:00:51.144 --> 00:01:00.714
Step three: start from the 0 on the BC side, follow that same scale round, and read where arm BA points. Here it's 50 degrees.

7
00:01:00.864 --> 00:01:11.994
Drawing an angle is similar: draw one arm, line up the centre and the zero line, mark a dot at the size you need, then join the vertex to the dot.

8
00:01:12.144 --> 00:01:19.674
Watch out! Some pupils read this angle as 130 degrees. They used the wrong scale!

9
00:01:19.824 --> 00:01:23.778
Use the scale that starts at 0 on the arm you lined up.

10
00:01:23.928 --> 00:01:33.882
Estimate first: this angle is smaller than a right angle, which is 90 degrees, so it can't be 130 degrees.

11
00:01:34.032 --> 00:01:45.810
Your turn! An angle has its vertex at Q, and its arms pass through P and R. How do we name it? Pause and think.

12
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:54.402
Angle PQR, or angle RQP. The vertex, Q, goes in the middle. Did you get it right?

13
00:01:54.552 --> 00:02:08.274
Another one! An angle is smaller than a right angle. The two scales show 40 and 140. Which is the answer? Pause and think.

14
00:02:08.424 --> 00:02:17.514
It's 40 degrees, because an angle smaller than a right angle must be less than 90 degrees. Did you get it right?

15
00:02:17.664 --> 00:02:27.402
Last one! To draw a 65 degree angle, what is the first step? Pause and think.

16
00:02:27.552 --> 00:02:41.538
First, draw one arm with a ruler. Then put the protractor's centre on the vertex, line the zero line up with the arm, mark a dot at 65, and join it up. Did you get it right?

17
00:02:41.688 --> 00:02:57.738
Remember: when naming an angle, put the vertex in the middle. To measure, put the centre on the vertex, line up the zero line with one arm, and read the scale that starts at 0 on that arm. Then estimate: is it smaller or bigger than a right angle?

18
00:02:57.888 --> 00:03:02.874
Look for angles at home and guess how big they are!

